{
  "id": "2026-09-16-history-native-configuration",
  "scope": "history",
  "kind": "review",
  "date": "2026-09-16",
  "question": "Should native Plite history own its configuration so the Plate HistoryPlugin defaults, state type and getter adapter can disappear?",
  "model": "GPT-6",
  "method": "best-api-review/history-v2",
  "trigger": "User challenges the Plate live-store getter adapter and explicitly asks whether Plite should be redesigned. This narrows and reconsiders the earlier blanket Stop.",
  "requirements": [
    "One owner for neutral history defaults, validation and configuration, one installed recorder, and predictable independent-editor lifetime.",
    "Preserve adopted replay, grouping, atomic publication and selection laws. If live parameter mutation is retained, preserve existing branches and grouping continuity without plugin replacement.",
    "Consider deleting the Plate wrapper, changing native configuration, adding a minimal neutral capability and moving ownership before defending the present boundary.",
    "Assess only this configuration proposal; do not implement a new public contract or treat live-store tests as proof of an independent application requirement."
  ],
  "evidenceReuse": "Rechecked live HistoryPlugin, native history options/validation/activation, native PluginDefinition and PluginPortal, Plate configuration/resolution/store owners, core installation and the public guide. Reused the shared-identity feasibility/production findings that Plate compilation has an independent product job. The earlier history behavior receipts remain baseline evidence, not proof of this proposed configuration design. Targeted application/source searches found live delay/depth changes in wrapper tests, not production application consumers.",
  "summary": "Pursue native history configuration ownership and deletion of the Plate-specific HistoryPlugin state/default/getter adapter. Supersede the prior blanket Stop only for this boundary: neutral settings currently require two declarations solely because native descriptors lack the Plate configuration capability. The strongest direction is one natively configurable descriptor that Plate consumes directly. A minimal shared initialState/store contract must compete with deleting the wrapper and keeping construction-time history(options); moving the entire Plate compiler/store system is not justified. Recommend $task design plan native history configuration: remove the Plate adapter.",
  "verdict": "pursue",
  "previous": "2026-09-16-history-post-adoption-reassessment",
  "relation": "supersedes",
  "alternatives": [
    "Keep getters: correct for the current live-store behavior, but duplicates defaults/types and coordinates each setting across layers without any Plate product policy.",
    "Remove Plate wrapper and use native history(options): strongest smaller alternative for construction-time configuration. This already gives the feature ownership of defaults, but does not itself supply a reusable configured descriptor or installed configuration contract. Design must compare this cut without assuming live mutation or the Plate spelling is a hard law.",
    "Native-owned configured descriptor consumed by both layers: strongest direction. Declare settings and defaults once, preserve inference and editor isolation, and remove the Plate state owner plus per-setting bridge. Minimal shared initialState/store support is a candidate; exact configuration spelling and live-update behavior remain design work.",
    "Feature-owned api.history.configure: may own live settings without a generic store, but forwarding the Plate store through it merely renames the bridge. It earns pursuit only if it eliminates the other owner and serves a concrete live configuration job.",
    "Move the entire Plate authoring/compiler/store/React system into Plite: reject on current evidence. Shared descriptor identity already coexists with a real Plate product compiler; two history parameters do not earn importing every product capability.",
    "Delete every Plate wrapper by analogy: reject. DefaultAuthoredPlugin supplies authenticated-user policy; the history adapter only forwards neutral settings. Configuration review does not reopen adopted replay/grouping or require another history engine."
  ],
  "proofLimits": "Bounded configuration review, not a new audit of all ten history units. Source inspection only; no product tests were added, removed or run, and no runtime/type-cost or native behavior claim is made for the proposed API. Existing tests express current live delay/retention behavior, not an established production requirement. Native activation resets automatic grouping time, so replacement cannot be assumed equivalent to parameter mutation. Design must settle defaults, validation timing, descriptor identity, disabled/default installation, editor isolation, lifecycle, inference and cost before adoption. Existing adopted/verified scope fields and prior receipts refer solely to implemented replay/grouping. Adoption of a common configuration contract requires scoped Best API and Plate/Plite plugin-authoring doctrine repair; no such repair is made in this read-only product assessment.",
